// The NAMED brace form ('{field = value}') is what the positional diagnostic
// tells callers to switch to, and it works fine on a LOCAL struct declarator.
// At global scope it shared the same silent-zero root cause as the positional
// form (found while enumerating the global-struct-positional-init issue): the
// scalar struct global path never applies field overrides to the Constant, so
// the brace values were discarded with no diagnostic either. There is no
// compile-time-constant construction path for this at global scope, so it is
// rejected too rather than silently losing the values.
expect_error("field initializers ('{field = value}') are not supported for struct type 'GpiNamed' at global scope") {
    struct GpiNamed { int x; int y; };
    GpiNamed gpiNamed = { x = 1, y = 2 };
}

// Same construct without '=' ('GpiNamed2 g {x=1,y=2};') - a different grammar node
// (barebraceList) carries the identical brace list; the guard must see this spelling
// too or the global still zeroes it, exactly as the filed bug did for the '=' spelling.
// (The bare spelling was not FULLY silent pre-fix the way '=' was: with no brace-list
// handling at all for it, 'right' stayed null, which also prints a non-blocking
// "struct and class is not initialized on the stack" note - noisy, but still wrong
// values, still exit 0, still no real diagnostic naming the construct.)
expect_error("field initializers ('{field = value}') are not supported for struct type 'GpiNamed2' at global scope") {
    struct GpiNamed2 { int x; int y; };
    GpiNamed2 gpiNamed2 { x = 1, y = 2 };
}
